WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
Hi, my name's Matthew Perrins.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.440
I'm part of the IBM Cloud team

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.880
and today I'm going to give you an introduction to Tekton.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.960
So, what is Tekton?

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.300
Tekton started life as part of the Knative Project

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.299
as they were trying to solve specific problems

00:00:15.299 --> 00:00:19.560
around how they do continuous
integration and continuous delivery of

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.540
their software components,

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.960
and was later donated to the Continuous Delivery Foundation

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.860
where we're now being able to

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.560
use it and consume it across multiple

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.680
Kubernetes environments and platforms.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:36.220
So, what I want to do today is give you

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.680
an overview of the base concepts

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.450
that underpin what Tekton is on,

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:46.440
explain about how you run it inside a
Kubernetes environment,

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.440
and how it helps you automate and deliver your software components

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.720
for running inside that platform.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.920
And then I want to give a brief explanation of how

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.160
developers would consume it and work with it

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:04.040
and how that separation of concerns may go forward.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:10.170
So, base level component of a
Tekton continuous integration or

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:16.970
delivery pipeline is something called a
task. And a task is an automation task,

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:24.170
yeah an automation tasks that you need
to create that will help you build, test,

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:32.040
deploy, manage, check the health of a
particular piece of software. Once you

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.150
create a number of tasks you can
actually isolate them and reuse them

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:40.619
very easily and Tekton one of the real
benefits is that it allows you to

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:46.200
separate your tasks that are then
consumed by your developers and I'll

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.490
explain that in a little bit more detail
as we move forward. So, once you've got a

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:59.399
base element task you can create
something called a pipeline and a

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:03.600
pipeline can be made up about any number of tasks

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.039
so you can customize that and tailor it
for the specific software needs that you

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:14.790
have. Once you've defined a pipeline you
can trigger a pipeline from something

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:22.319
called a pipeline run, and you create
multiple runs in a software delivery

00:02:22.319 --> 00:02:27.450
project but it will continue to run the
exact same definition of the pipeline

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:31.860
that you defined with the exact same
definition of the task that you define.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:37.410
Now one of the next pieces of key
information to make this sequence run

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:43.890
smoothly is you need to provide some
data for that execution so that you can

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:50.340
tailor and customize it. Anything from a
build number to supplying a unique git

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:55.970
repository or supplying a Docker image
and this is called

00:02:55.970 --> 00:03:05.730
Pipeline Resource Res. So, now we built
these base components for a Tekton

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:10.049
Pipeline what we need to do is then
start to think about how we prepare a

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:15.450
developer to consume them. So, what you would typically do is you'd register your

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:22.320
common tasks into your Kubernetes
environment inside the Tekton Execution

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:27.450
Engine that you will install inside Kubernetes, and those tasks can be commonly

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:33.090
shared across different applications or
different development teams. So, it allows

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:37.709
a level of management and control that
can be owned by the operation, the

00:03:37.709 --> 00:03:44.310
operations side of your DevOps
organization. So, Tekton itself runs

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:50.250
inside the Kubernetes cluster and it
associates very cleanly to a lot of the

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:59.459
Kubernetes concepts like pods and
services and CRD, CRC, YAML definitions. So,

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:03.989
once you've got your pipelines defined
or the concepts about how you're going

00:04:03.989 --> 00:04:09.180
to use your pipelines and once you've
actually installed and got Tekton

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.290
running inside your Kubernetes environment,
what we need to do is then talk about

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:17.109
how a developer
would start using that in the creation

00:04:17.109 --> 00:04:23.680
and delivery of their projects. So, as I
mentioned you can have different Tekton

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:34.390
pipeline definitions and we'll call that
pipeline YAML and you can define that

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:40.120
file inside your individual application
and the relationship if you remember is

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:45.820
a pipeline can run any number of tasks
and these tasks have been already

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:52.450
registered and defined inside the Tekton
environment. So, you can have task 1, task

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:56.650
2, task 3, and keep going,
and within each task you can have a

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:03.070
number of steps and again you can have
any number of steps depending on the

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:05.080
particular problem you're trying to
solve.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:10.810
So once you've defined your pipeline
definition inside your application then

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:19.570
the developer can push their change to
their Git Repository and this will allow

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:25.690
them to iterate multiple times on code
changes or definition changes or feature

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:32.979
and function improvements. And what
happens is this triggers a webhook which

00:05:32.979 --> 00:05:37.840
will then trigger a type a Tekton
execution which will then marry the two

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:42.190
elements together, it will marry their
pipeline definition that comes from the

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:47.200
application and it will take the tasks
that you've defined inside your Tekton

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:53.490
environment and this could result and we
hope it does successful delivery of your

00:05:53.490 --> 00:06:01.000
applications into specific namespaces,
whether it be a dev, a test, or a pre-prod.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:14.200
So, you've actually packaged up your code
into a K8, a K8 image registry and from

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:17.979
there you've now got a piece of packaged
software that you can then roll out and

00:06:17.979 --> 00:06:23.860
deploy to other environments. So, I hope
you enjoyed that walkthrough and

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:27.729
you've got some information about the
basic concepts of what Tekton

00:06:27.729 --> 00:06:33.009
is, how applications can use it, and how
you run it on top of the Kubernetes

00:06:33.009 --> 00:06:38.110
platform. Thank you if you have any
questions please drop us a line below. If

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:42.189
you want to see more videos like this in
the future, please like and subscribe and

00:06:42.189 --> 00:06:46.569
don't forget you can always get started
on the cloud at no cost by signing up

00:06:46.569 --> 00:06:50.610
for a free IBM Cloud account.

